# Authentication and explicit administration

Set a random `AUTH_SESSION_SECRET` of at least 32 characters. No user or password is
provided by default. Missing authentication configuration makes login return 503.

For local development, set `AUTH_LOCAL_USERNAME`, `AUTH_LOCAL_PASSWORD_HASH`,
`AUTH_LOCAL_ROLES` and `AUTH_LOCAL_PERMISSIONS`. Generate a password hash with
`python -m app.adapters.local_identity` inside the generated service environment;
the password is read interactively and is not a command-line argument. The hash format
uses colons, so it does not trigger Compose dollar interpolation. Over local HTTP,
explicitly set `AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=false`. Use the default `true` with HTTPS.

Sign in at `/auth/login`. Authentication uses signed, readable session cookies with
a maximum lifetime of 15 minutes; tokens and passwords are not stored in them. Logging
out clears the browser cookie; it does not revoke a previously stolen stateless cookie.
Do not put confidential profile data in session fields. Local login has a process-wide
attempt budget; multiple workers require a shared ingress rate limit.

The authorization modes are `authentication`, `roles` (admin role), and `permissions`
(admin role plus named permission). Admin always needs authentication and only the
resources selected in `admin.resources` are registered. In permissions mode grant
`admin:access` and the relevant `records:read`, `records:write`, `records:delete`,
`schedules:read` or `schedules:write` permissions. Mutating admin forms require CSRF.

OIDC uses Authlib's authorization-code flow with state, nonce and PKCE. Configure
`OIDC_ISSUER`, `OIDC_CLIENT_ID`, `OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET` and the explicit HTTPS
`OIDC_REDIRECT_URI` ending in `/auth/callback`. Remote admin identities must appear in
`AUTH_ADMIN_SUBJECTS`; roles and permissions come from verified issuer claims.

Firebase uses the Google sign-in web flow and verifies Firebase ID tokens with RS256
and Google's public JWKS, including issuer, audience, expiry and auth_time. Configure
`FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`, `FIREBASE_WEB_API_KEY`, `FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN` and allowed admin
subjects. Enable Google sign-in and the application's domain in Firebase. The web API
key is public Firebase client configuration, not a server service-account credential.
This adapter does not check Firebase account revocation/disablement on every request;
short session expiration is enforced. External identity flows need verification with
your own provider configuration; the jobs/admin integration tests use local auth.
